---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { liveTools } from '../lib/tools';
---

<Base
  title="OpenDrawer — free PDF tools that never upload your files"
  description="Free, open-source PDF tools that run entirely in your browser. Merge, split, inspect and edit PDFs without uploading them. No account, no ads, no file-size limit."
>
  <div class="stack-lg">
    <section class="stack" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
      <p class="tag">Free · open source · no account</p>
      <h1 id="hero-heading">Document tools that never upload your files</h1>
      <p>
        OpenDrawer runs in your browser. The PDF you choose is read on your own device, by your own
        processor. There is no server to send it to, so there is nothing to trust us with.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/tools/inspect-pdf">Inspect a PDF</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack" id="tools" aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
      <h2 id="tools-heading">Tools</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        One tool is built. The rest of the suite is being added in order, each with its limits
        written on its own page.
      </p>
      <ul class="card-grid">
        {
          liveTools.map((tool) => (
            <li class="card">
              <h3><a href={`/tools/${tool.id}`}>{tool.name}</a></h3>
              <p>{tool.blurb}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="stack" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
      <h2 id="privacy-heading">How to check that nothing is uploaded</h2>
      <p>
        You do not have to believe a privacy policy. Open the developer tools in your browser
        (F12, or Cmd+Option+I on a Mac), switch to the Network tab, and then use a tool. No request
        leaves this page. You can also switch off Wi-Fi after the page loads: the tools keep
        working.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is built so that this stays true. Its Content-Security-Policy only permits
        requests to its own origin, so a future change that tried to send your file somewhere else
        would be blocked by the browser rather than trusted to good behaviour.
      </p>
      <div class="note">
        <h3>What is not here</h3>
        <p>
          Office documents to PDF, PDF/A archiving, and certified e-signatures are not offered.
          They need software that cannot run reliably in a browser, and pretending otherwise would
          waste your time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
